"mermaid-diagram usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		Mermaid diagram
		<header>A Mermaid diagram draws a flowchart, a sequence, a timeline or any other kind of diagram Mermaid knows, from the element's own text or a file on this site.</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  what to write, where Mermaid comes from, the events, keyboard and accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write Mermaid's text inside `<ui-mermaid-diagram>`:  every kind of diagram Mermaid has today draws, with
			nothing to turn on.  The text comes from, first match wins:

			- `source`:  a file of this site (same origin only), fetched when `load` says.
			- A `<script type="text/mermaid">` child:  its text exactly, so `<`, `>` and `&` need no escaping.
			- The element's own text, dedented.

			It draws again when the text changes:  typing in the editor, a script setting `content`, or the file
			changing.  A setting written in the diagram's own text (its front matter, or a `%%{init: ...}%%` line)
			wins over the attributes, as it does on GitHub.

			In `<ui-markdown>`, a ````mermaid```` block draws as a diagram too.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Loading">
		<markdown>
			Mermaid is big, so it loads only the first time a page draws a diagram, in files of its own:  a page without
			one pays nothing.  Mermaid then loads only the part for each kind of diagram it draws.  ELK, the layout
			engine for flowcharts, state, class and ER diagrams, loads once a page, when one needs it.

			Mermaid draws in its strict mode:  HTML in a label shows as text, and clicks that would run code are off,
			so it's safe with text you didn't write.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-drawn` -- drawn:  `{ diagramType }`, what Mermaid read the text as (`flowchart-v2`, `sequence` ...).
			- `ui-error` (cancelable) -- loading or drawing failed:  `{ kind, source, error }`, `kind` `render` for text
			  Mermaid can't read.  Cancel it to skip the message.
			- `ui-loaded`, `ui-changed`, `ui-saving`, `ui-saved` -- the text, shared with `<ui-code>` and `<ui-markdown>`.

			The drawing itself is the `svg` property, as SVG text, for copying or saving;  `diagramType` and `error`
			say what was drawn, or what went wrong.  `putBack()` forgets a reader's picks from the menu.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const diagram = document.querySelector("ui-mermaid-diagram")
			diagram.addEventListener("ui-drawn", (event) => console.log("drew a", event.detail.diagramType))
			diagram.content = "flowchart LR\n  a --> b"
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- With `controls`, **Tab** reaches the menu button, which shows at once;  **Enter** or **Space** opens it,
			  and the arrow keys move through the themes and light or dark.
			- Without it, the drawing is no tab stop:  there's nothing in it to use.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The drawing is an image (`role="img"`), named by `label`;  without one, by the title in the diagram's own
			  text (`accTitle`, or a `title`);  else by its kind ("Flowchart diagram").  Give a `label` that says what
			  the diagram shows.
			- An error is a `<ui-message>`, one short line per thing:  what went wrong, the line, what was expected.
			- The menu's words are translated, with the rest of the tag's.
		</>
	</>
